<div class="eng"><div class="lab">GESHE MICHAEL'S ENGLISH (VERBATIM)</div>
<span class="u" data-d="1" data-l="s200S">Because you have <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s200w1">lied</span>, you live in a world where, when you undertake <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s200w2">farming</span> or some work in cooperation with other people, in the end the work <span class="u" data-d="3" data-l="s200p1">fails to prosper</span>, and the people <span class="u" data-d="3" data-l="s200p2">can&#x27;t work well together</span>, and <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s200w3">for the most part</span> everyone is <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s200w4">cheating</span> one another, and is <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s200w5">afraid</span>, and where there are <span class="u" data-d="5" data-l="s200w6">many</span> <span class="u" data-d="3" data-l="s200p3">things to be afraid of</span>.</span>
</div>
</div>
<div class="note"><b>AI NOTE</b> &mdash; Provisional machine alignment by Codex (English-first proposal). The cooperative-work unit is omitted under Rule 10 because source GRU (‘ship’) conflicts with GMR's cooperation wording and the likely GROGS reading is only a proposed erratum. The two negative-result predicates are retained only as complete phrase recasts; their auxiliaries are not assigned false word-level exponents. Explicit English order selects the fear occurrence after ‘cheating’ and places ‘many’ before the later fear phrase. Conjunctions, participants, and the final possessive construction remain unwrapped.</div>
